    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d39">

                <pb facs="0320" n="34" xml:id="pg34"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/152a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Germany</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gerard</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-05-26T14:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 6, 1916, 2 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3062. Berlin press dispatch dated May 26 quotes you as having given interview
                    with Munich newspaper on favorable prospects of peace. Berlin dispatch dated
                    June 3 reports interview with evening <hi rendition="#italic">National
                        Zeitung</hi> on same subject. Department assumes that these reports are mere
                    inventions, but would be glad to have some statement from you to this effect in
                    order that interviews may be officially denied here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
